  {
    "episode_id": 72659,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Sure. Well, so before the legislature went into recess, we passed a bill called SB 89 that gave the governor a billion dollars of appropriation authority to deal with stopping the spread of coronavirus. And under that bill, what the governor was, was required to do is submit notice to the joint legislative budget committee under section 36 of the bill, which is called a section 36 notice of his intent to spend those funds. So that's placing extraordinary trust in the governor. I think in a lot of cases, the governor respected that trust. And there were very few of those section 36 letters that I personally objected to, but there were a couple. And the ones that I objected to were the ones where I felt he was exceeding the authority that we had given him. We gave him authority to deal with the spread of coronavirus. However, Everyone acknowledged that there were going to be huge challenges that the state would face in recovering economically from the crisis and socially from the crisis. And in SB 89, we made it very clear to the executive branch that the legislature wanted to be an equal partner in figuring out what those solutions were. And I think that the governor in some ways went way beyond the authority that we gave him. I think it was well intentioned, but we are a co-equal branch of government. We have a constitutional role to play an oversight of that spending authority, and we wanted to be including in those decisions. In some instances, we were not. And that frustration is not just mine. I mean, it was a bicameral, bipartisan frustration with the governor for not including the legislature more in those spending decisions.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
