    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's a little surreal. We have been unable to reach agreement with the Senate on a plan for being able to legislate remotely. We have some constitutional concerns about our ability to make remote votes. The Senate is trying very hard to protect all of the members of the legislature from contacting coronavirus since quite a few of the members are in high risk medical categories. So it's been a challenge. So when we have hearings, you'll see us in socially distanced rooms. So we're using only the largest of our committee rooms, normally to every third seat that's occupied. So we're all spread out throughout the room. We wear masks, except perhaps when we need to present. And we're, we're careful to disinfect all the surfaces, all the microphones have, have covers on them. So we're trying to be very cautious and strike a balance between the need to protect the health of not only the legislature, legislators, but our staff, but also to respect our need to conduct the public's business, because obviously, time has run very short for us budgetarily. The Constitution requires us to pass a budget by June 15th, and that date is fast approaching. So, you know, this is far from normal legislative calendar in terms of crafting the budget.",
