  {
    "episode_id": 72659,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "Well, I haven't heard a lot of legislative tax increases. However, we're certainly aware, as your listeners are, I'm sure, of the tax increases that have been proposed by the governor, because there are a couple that increased tax on vaping. There are some changes to business taxes that will result in tax increases. The disallowance of net operating losses for medium and large businesses is one of them. The disallowance of use of more than $5 million of tax credits is another. I don't think that that's fair. I mean, I think that if we pass a tax credit and we say, okay, look, if you do this, we'll give you a credit. And if people have planned for that, that it's unfair to say, oh, by the way, you know what, we were just kidding. You can only use a certain percentage of those this year because we have a budget problem. In addition, though, I think we all have to be cognizant of the changes to Prop 13 that are being proposed on the ballot in November. This is the so-called split-role tax provision that would limit the effect of Prop 13 to just residential properties and not commercial properties. In other words, so commercial properties would no longer enjoy the tax protections of Prop 13. And this is something I'm very much opposed to. I'm a believer in Prop 13. I think that in 1978, the voters of California drew a line in the sand and said, this is the maximum amount of tax that's reasonable to assess against property and no more. And I think that if we allow it to be weakened, it's going to be just taking a break out of the wall and the whole wall is going to collapse. we're going to lose those protections as we move forward. So it's something that I've been telling people to be very aware of that we need to make sure that people will know on that in November.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
