    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, I mean, I think anyone that's looking to get something out of the budget is going to be disappointed. The best that people can hope for is not to have cuts made. And I'll tell you, a lot of my time over the last couple of weeks has been fielding calls from people all over the state organizations, nonprofits, social programs saying, please, please, please, legislature, do not cut our budget. And it's heartbreaking because these people represent real needs in our in our communities, and yet we've got this huge $54 billion budget shortfall that we have to deal with. So it's gonna be a problem, it's gonna be painful. I think that the guiding principles that we need to observe is, first of all, we need to make sure that we don't make budget cuts that are going to prevent the state from recovering economically. And some examples of that would be, you know, we certainly don't want to cut healthcare. That's something that people are going to need. We're in the middle of an epidemic. So that's important. Also, I think we need to be cognizant that we need to help our businesses recover because businesses where jobs come from and a lot of people are out of work and a lot of our businesses are hurt against, especially our small businesses. So we need to make sure that we pay attention to things like unemployment insurance. We've taken massive multi-billion dollars What are called title 12 loans to shore up from the federal government to shore up unemployment system. We need to make sure we budget for the state paying that back in the future. The last time the state took those loans, the state failed to pay them back and the federal government assessed penalties for years against every business in the state. In fact, it was until 2018 that we finally ended up paying that back. And then the other thing is, we need to recognize that the businesses have furloughed employees because state government required them to. It's not their fault. In December, the Employment Development Department is going to recalculate the unemployment insurance rates for every employer in California for the next budget year. And we need to make sure that we hold those employers harmless. We can't let those rates go way up, just when those businesses are trying to recover for something that wasn't their fault.",
