  {
    "episode_id": 72659,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 27,
    "turn_text": "Well, you know, most of my bills are usually increasing the efficiency of government, especially using technology to empower those increases. So I've got those bills. We paired them back to just the bills that are uncontroversial because I don't want to take up committee time with them. So most of my bills are bills that were designed to go through on what we call consent, which means that they don't actually have to be heard. It just gets tagged with a yes vote for everybody because of the uncontroversial nature. I've got a couple that I am passionate about. I would really like to continue increasing the number of judgeships in California. We're short by hundreds of judges statewide, and the highest judicial need are in the sections of the state that I represent. So that's something that will that I'd like to see done. So I'm trying to add 25 judges. That's an uphill battle this year because of the budget crisis. So, but that's one that I still think is important to have a discussion about. And also there's bills that we have, as we say, they have urgency, which means that they have to be done this year. I've got one of those, the junior hunting license program is expiring at the end of the year. In fact, at the end of the summer, if we don't renew it, it will expire. And so that's something we have to take action on now. And so that's one of the bills that I have.",
