  {
    "episode_id": 72659,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Yes, I have. I talk to my colleagues at the federal government and in Congress quite often. Obviously, Governor Newsom has structured his budget proposal with federal triggers so that he's outlined cuts that will be made unless the federal government steps in with $14 billion, at least the money for California. And I think it's unwise actually to count on help from the federal government, just because the federal government has a responsibility to tax payers the same way that we do. And they're looking at a federal deficit and a federal debt that is approaching the largest in the history of our country, even if you account for the size of our gross domestic product. So the largest the debt has ever been. It's been at the end of World War II. And with cares and cares to the current round of stimulus that's passed Congress. If you count those in, we're almost up to that same level of debt. And the Heroes Act passed the House of Representatives last week. If that were to pass the Senate and be signed by the President, we would actually be above that level of debt. And so you have to take that very seriously. And so that's a difficult decision for them. something that they're going to be grappling with. I wouldn't count on that. Certainly, we're not going to be able to count on that for the budget this year. It has to be passed by June 15th, which means it has to be crafted substantially before then. And I just don't think that Congress is going to act on another round of stimulus before them.",
