  {
    "episode_id": 72659,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 35,
    "turn_text": "Well, honestly, we've suspended the campaign for the time being. We have bigger issues to face as a state. I have a large responsibility, not only to my constituents in the 33rd Assembly District, but as Vice Chair of the Budget Committee with the largest budget deficit in state history. And that's picking up all of my time. So we'll pick up the campaign again when we get on the other side of the budget. And as some of the state home orders are relaxed, we can start having events again, start fundraising again. But you know what, that really doesn't bother me. That's in keeping with my general philosophy on campaigning. We, in the assembly, have to run for re-election every two years the same way that we have to in Congress. And we have a primary, mandatory primary every two years. So that's an election a year on average. And if you focus on campaigning instead of doing your job, it'll drive you crazy. So I've always figured that we're elected to do the best job we can. That includes getting out in front of our constituents, listening to them, getting to know what their problems are, and then coming to Sacramento and trying to solve their problems. If we're doing those things, then we're already campaigning. And if we're not doing those things that we frankly don't deserve to get reelected. So those are the things that I'm focusing on. And I'm sure we'll pick the congressional campaign back up here in another couple of months as the state gets further down the road to recovery.",
