    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72659.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "Well, I mean, it's fundamentally altered a lot of business in California. And frankly, I think some of those alterations are permanent, you know, for better or for worse. I think people are realizing that working from home is a viable option for a lot of different industries. I think people's eyes have been opened to the value of virtual meetings. to the value of not having to drive places and all the concomitant time that that wastes. However, I think it also has a downside. I think some of the jobs that have been lost to automation are not going to come back. So if you look at some of the projections, we think that our economic recovery could be relatively quick, but we think that our employment recovery is going to take years. It's going to take at least two years to get back up to the level of employment that we had before the coronavirus hit. So, you know, it's a double-edged sword, but, you know, hopefully in hindsight, you know, when we get 10 years down the road, we'll look back and say it was that that part of the impact was positive for the application of technology to making our industries more efficient.",
